    "name": "Speed tail-sitter",
    "blurb": "Four motors along the nose, a body that flies like a wing, and nothing else. Stands on its tail, pitches over, and runs.",
    "notes": "A tail-sitter in the class of the Peregreen V4, not a copy of it. Published: 657.59 km/h (408.6 mph) Guinness record, four T-Motor 3120 at 900KV, props cut down to 6 inches, LiPo built for short bursts, 3D printed. The V3 weighed 2.77 kg. Assumed here: 2.8 kg all-up; 5.1 kgf of thrust per motor on a full 12S pack (7.3:1); 260 m/s pitch speed, which is what a 6-inch prop cut from a high-pitch blade gives at about 38,000 rpm; 0.0017 m2 of head-on drag area, a drag coefficient of 0.24 on the 95 mm body. That last one is the number doing the work: it is the only one that cannot be looked up, and it was chosen so the model reaches the published record. It lands at 649 km/h, 1.3% under. The fuselage is modelled as a lifting body (a low aspect ratio surface with a slow lift curve and a late stall) and the four arms as a cruciform wing at 45 degrees. No compressibility: at 650 km/h this is Mach 0.53 with the prop tips past Mach 1, so treat the top end as optimistic.",
